@*
    Dashboard-specific head content, contributed to AppBase through
    DocumentOptions.AddHeadComponent<DashboardHead>() in DashboardSiteOptions.

    Everything structural — charset, viewport, <base href>, stylesheets, the scoped-CSS bundle,
    ImportMap, HeadOutlet — is emitted by AppBase. What remains here is what only the dashboard
    knows: the tenant's brand colour, the server-rendered colour scheme, the zero-flicker
    handshake that MudBlazor's server-side theming needs, the tenant favicon, and the consumer's
    custom snippet.

    No <title> here on purpose. PageHead emits it through HeadOutlet for every routed page,
    already composed with the tenant's website title — a raw <title> in this component would
    render earlier in the document and win, permanently freezing every page on the site name.
*@
@namespace Zonit.Dashboard.Components
@using Zonit.Extensions.Tenants

@inject ITenantProvider Tenant
@inject IDashboardCurrentSite Dashboard

@*
    No <meta name="color-scheme"> here any more, and no IThemeManager injection either.

    Injecting it made this component — which lives in the HEAD, and AppBase builds the head before
    the body — the first thing in the circuit to resolve the scoped ThemeManager. That happened
    before WebsiteHydrator (body) had restored the cookie jar, so the manager resolved against an
    empty jar, decided Auto/light, and the page rendered light under a server that had rendered
    dark. The correction one render later is the flash.

    Nothing is lost by dropping the meta: DashboardThemeRoot emits
    "color-scheme: dark|light !important" on :root, and a CSS declaration on the root element
    overrides the meta tag anyway — so the meta was already being ignored by the browser.
*@

@* Zero-flicker dark-mode handshake. Runs BEFORE Blazor boots:
     1. Detects prefers-color-scheme.
     2. Stamps the result into a cookie so the next SSR can render the matching palette without
        a round-trip to JS (no flash-of-light).
     3. Paints a matching background on <html> immediately so the browser does not show its
        default white between the first byte and MudBlazor's CSS loading. Once MudThemeProvider
        mounts it owns the palette and the inline style is overridden cleanly.

   This is deliberately NOT the framework's AppearanceScript. That one is purely client-side and
   keeps the HTML identical for every visitor, which is what makes a public site cacheable.
   MudThemeProvider resolves its palette on the SERVER, so the dashboard has to tell the server
   what the visitor's system preference is — hence the cookie. The dashboard is behind a login
   and never cached, so it pays nothing for that. *@
<script>
    (function () {
        try {
            var prefersDark = !!(window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches);
            var cookie = function (n) { return (document.cookie.match(new RegExp('(?:^|; )' + n + '=([^;]*)')) || [])[1]; };

            var mode = cookie('ui\\.mode');
            var seen = cookie('ui\\.scheme');
            var actual = prefersDark ? '1' : '0';

            if (seen !== actual) {
                document.cookie = 'ui.scheme=' + actual + '; path=/; max-age=31536000; SameSite=Lax';
            }

            //  One reload, only when the server had NOTHING to go on.
            //
            //  Measured on WebKit at an iPhone profile: with Auto and no ui.scheme cookie the
            //  first load renders light and flips to dark at 163ms, because the server cannot
            //  know a client-only media query it has never been told. Loads two and three of the
            //  same session are stable. So this is not the every-refresh flash — it is the very
            //  first visit, once per browser.
            //
            //  Scoped to ABSENT, not to disagreement. An earlier revision reloaded whenever the
            //  cookie merely differed, which on a browser that does not commit a cookie write
            //  before the navigation that follows it turns into a flip on every single load —
            //  the reported symptom, caused by the attempted cure.
            //
            //  sessionStorage is a hard one-shot: even if the cookie write never lands, this can
            //  run at most once per tab, so it cannot become a reload loop.
            if (!seen && mode !== 'Dark' && mode !== 'Light' && !sessionStorage.getItem('ui.boot')) {
                try { sessionStorage.setItem('ui.boot', '1'); } catch (e) { }
                window.location.replace(window.location.href);
                return;
            }

            //  NO reload here any more, and removing it is the point.
            //
            //  A previous revision reloaded the page whenever it found ui.scheme disagreed with
            //  the real preference, so the server could re-render from a corrected cookie. On a
            //  browser that does not commit a document.cookie write before the navigation that
            //  follows it, that turns into: paint with the server's value, navigate, paint again
            //  with the corrected one — a visible flip on EVERY load rather than only on the rare
            //  one where the preference actually changed. It was a guess, it made the reported
            //  symptom worse, and it is gone.
            //
            //  Auto is therefore resolved from whatever ui.scheme held at request time. On the
            //  first ever visit, or the first load after the system preference changes, the
            //  server can be one request behind; the cookie written just above fixes the next
            //  one. That is the honest cost of resolving a client-only media query on a server.
            //  First paint, before any stylesheet has loaded. Values track the themes' own
            //  Background slots; they were left behind on an older palette and painted #161723
            //  under a page whose real background had moved to #0a0d12.
            var isDark = mode === 'Dark' || (mode !== 'Light' && prefersDark);
            document.documentElement.style.backgroundColor = isDark ? '#0a0d12' : '#f4f6fa';
            document.documentElement.style.colorScheme = isDark ? 'dark' : 'light';
        } catch (e) { /* failures here must never block render */ }
    })();
</script>

@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Tenant.Settings.Site.FaviconUrl))
{
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="@(Tenant.Settings.Site.FaviconUrl)" />
}

@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Dashboard.CustomSnippet))
{
    @((MarkupString)Dashboard.CustomSnippet!)
}
